> When you ask to draw some text, you specify the font size in points:
> e.g. Times-Roman 9 pt.  A point is defined as 1/72 of an inch, so the
> nominal height of Times-Roman 9 pt is 0.125 inches. (Individual
> characters vary of course; "p" has a descender, "h" has an ascender,
> etc.  The "nominal height" can usually be thought of as the height of
> the letter "M".)
> In order to know how many pixels to use when drawing the font, the X
> server has to know how many pixels make up an inch -- then it can
> divide by 72 and multiply by 9 to get the number of pixels that one
> character of the font will take up.
> Saying that a standard Linux font is at 75 dpi means that the X Server
> will assume there are 75 pixels to an inch when rendering that font. If
> in fact the display is a 100dpi display, that means that there are 100
> pixels to the inch, and the X Server will end up drawing the font
> uncomfortably small.  This is why "Install 100 dpi fonts" is an option
> when you set up Linux.

I didn't know that Linux handled it that way, thanks for explaining!=20
However, the basic thought of this system described (slightly) flawed,=20
though I can see how it works to a certain degree for the desktop=20
display scenario.
The *surface* dpi is not usually what you are ultimately interested in=20
(even though it may be a useful shorthand for a certain type of viewing=20
setup), the actual result you want is on the retina of the viewer, which=20
is particularly well exemplified by the OP here (and why this came up).
That is what makes the OP need a high-res font for a 60 dpi display=20
(which would for someone not considering all pertinent circumstances=20
seem like a paradox), and that is also what the responding=20
'MassiveProng' missed (besides the difference between 1080p and 1080=20
dpi) in his response.

A more complete and general way of creating a system for selecting font=20
resolution in pixels would be to take into account, together with=20
physical display area and pixel resolution, the viewing distance.
But then it would still only be an estimate since personal preferences=20
vary greatly. For example, I prefer my 9 pt sans serifs on my 100 dpi=20
(1600x1200 20") display, most others think this makes the letters much=20
too small, some even preferring a 1024x768 pixel resolution (64 dpi) for=20
the same physical display size. For me, being used to working at high-
dpi displays, it's extremely awkward to use (at the normal desktop=20
viewing distance) <80 dpi displays with standard font sizes.
